> I wonder, doesn't the compiler enforce these non-null fields to be initialized inline or in the constructor? How does this PR deal with that?

For regular C# class yes, but not for hybrid class (aka the script we attaches to a node or the future C# type we use to interop with the Engine). For these classes, we some times do not initialize them via a constructor, instead, we initialize them via the `_Ready` method with "expectations" on certain exported parameters already set, this PR focuses on ensuring these expectations.

Before this PR, with nullable set to off, you get no information on whether an exported reference type is null, and you ensure its nullability by disipline. With nullable set to on, you get one `Non-nullable property must contain a non-null value when exiting constructor. Consider declaring the property as nullable` warning for each exported field you declare, for large projects, every compile or syntax analysis produces hundreds of these warnings that floods your IDE or build output, renders the `Nullable` effectively an unusable feature.

```csharp
public partial class Main : Node
{
#nullable enable
    [Export] private string _stringValue; // Non-nullable property must contain a non-null value when exiting constructor. Consider declaring the property as nullable
    [Export] private StringName _stringNameValue; // Non-nullable property must contain a non-null value when exiting constructor. Consider declaring the property as nullable
    [Export] private NodePath _nodePathValue; // Non-nullable property must contain a non-null value when exiting constructor. Consider declaring the property as nullable
    [Export] private Node _nodeValue; // Non-nullable property must contain a non-null value when exiting constructor. Consider declaring the property as nullable
    [Export] private Resource _resourceValue; // Non-nullable property must contain a non-null value when exiting constructor. Consider declaring the property as nullable
    [Export] private Dictionary<int, Node> _dictionaryValue; // Non-nullable property must contain a non-null value when exiting constructor. Consider declaring the property as nullable
    [Export] private Array<string> _arrayValue; // Non-nullable property must contain a non-null value when exiting constructor. Consider declaring the property as nullable
    [Export] private int[] _intValue; // Non-nullable property must contain a non-null value when exiting constructor. Consider declaring the property as nullable
}
```

For developers that really wants Nullable to be enabled without these compiler warnings, they have to do the following to "suppress" the analysis, which causes these exported fields' nullability hardlocks to "notnull".

```csharp
public partial class Main : Node
{
#nullable enable
    [Export] private string _stringValue = null!;
    [Export] private StringName _stringNameValue  = null!;
    [Export] private NodePath _nodePathValue  = null!;
    [Export] private Node _nodeValue  = null!;
    [Export] private Resource _resourceValue  = null!;
    [Export] private Dictionary<int, Node> _dictionaryValue  = null!;
    [Export] private Array<string> _arrayValue  = null!;
    [Export] private int[] _intValue  = null!;
    [Export] private string[] _stringArrayValue  = null!;
}
```

However, on the design perspective, whether these fields are null or not null really should depends on whether the developer has assigned these values from the inspector, suppressing them simply mean `if the developer forgets to assign these values, they will cause NullReferenceExceptions on their call sites`, effectively equals disabling nullability.

This PR does exactly that: When the developer has enabled nullability in their project, and have `<GodotEnableExportNullChecks>true</GodotEnableExportNullChecks>` enabled, the analyzer shipped with this PR tells the C# nullability analyzer to assume these fields are not null at compile time, but throws Exceptions when the target hybrid types has finishes initialized by GodotEngine in runtime (after godot news the type, assigned all its export accroding to tres or tscn).

The Unity Engine is using similar approach, but throws `MissingReferenceException` instead.

```csharp
public partial class Main : Node
{
#nullable enable
    [Export] private string _stringValue;
    [Export] private StringName _stringNameValue;
    [Export] private NodePath _nodePathValue;
    [Export] private Node _nodeValue;
    [Export] private Resource _resourceValue; // Not set in scene editor's inspector, cause exception when Godot loads this scene.
    [Export] private Dictionary<int, Node> _dictionaryValue;
    [Export] private Array<string> _arrayValue ;
    [Export] private int[] _intValue;
    [Export] private string[] _stringArrayValue;
}
```

> It seems this relies on a pattern of lying to the compiler about the nullability of these fields. Non-nullables are meant to be non-null always and everywhere, but this PR asserts these fields will be null after construction (and thus within the constructors) until Godot actually assigns the properties. EDIT: Did I understand this correctly or is this not how this PR works?

Mostly correct, but the overal idea will sounds wrong when you are putting it this way out from its original context. Rather than lying to the compiler, we instead guarentees the compiler that, when the life time of any of these fields are active, they will be in a not-null state, the only difference is that we have an additional initialization step before these fields enter their life time scope. You may find this conversation I had with dotnet/roslyn team member when trying to seeking a way of achieve such behavior: https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/issues/6471#issuecomment-3467582375, and if you following down the proposal that feature: [DiagnosticSuppressorDesign.md](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/main/docs/analyzers/DiagnosticSuppressorDesign.md), you may find that this exact feature is just the feature Unity is requesting for their same mechanism: https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/issues/30172 **EDIT: And has shipped by unity since 2018.4 [Unity Forum Link](https://discussions.unity.com/t/suggesting-solutions-for-serializefield-warning/806898/40)**

> Serialization frameworks and tools embedding .NET Core or Mono often set the value of fields or call methods outside of user code.
> This leads the IDE to show warning about unused code while the user of the framework knows the fields and methods are going to be used. One example of this scenario is Unity. A typical game object will look like this:
```csharp
using UnityEngine;

class BulletMovement : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private float speed;

    private void Update()
    {
         this.transform.position *= speed;
    }
}
```
> In that case:

> - the value of the field speed is set by Unity based on the value set in the Unity Editor.
> - the method Update is going to be invoked by Unity at each and every frame of the game.
> For the Tools for Unity, we'd like a way to suppress the warning CS0649 for the field.

So in short, I believe this approach, aka lying to the complier, or make customized check on top of the nullability analyzer, is widely adopted by C# framewoks, and is common practice.

> If the constructor tries to use the field, what will happen? Compiler warning/error or runtime NullReferenceException? I assume the latter, because how can we even determined some other method that accesses the field is not being called from the constructor?

FWIW this is just how nullable work. The following would NRE anywhere, but is still valid as far as nullability is concerned.

```cs
public class Foo
{
    private Bar bar;
    
    public Foo()
    {
        bar.SayHello(); // Should "never be null" according to annotations.
        
        bar = new Bar();
    }
}

public class Bar
{
    public void SayHello()
    {
        System.Console.WriteLine("Hello World!");
    }
}
```

I just checked with the following sample:

```cs
using System;

#nullable enable

public class Program
{
	public string StrField;
	
	public Program()
	{
		MethodThatAccessesStrField();
		StrField = "aa";
	}
	
	private void MethodThatAccessesStrField()
	{
		Console.WriteLine(StrField.Length);
	}
	
	public static void Main()
	{
		_ = new Program();
	}
}
```

Without assigning `StrField` in the constructor, you get this warning: `Program.cs(9,9): warning CS8618: Non-nullable field 'StrField' must contain a non-null value when exiting constructor. Consider declaring the field as nullable.`

However, if you initialize the `StrField` after the call to `MethodThatAccessesStrField()`, you get no warning, despite that method accessing the field. This results in a `NullReferenceException` at runtime without any compiler warning.

This is a loophole in C#'s nullable system, which does not surprise me as I was already aware of a different one. C#'s nullable system does not guarantee at 100% that you will not get a `NullReferenceException` at runtime, unlike other languages (Kotlin, Rust, Swift, etc).

Assuming that this loophole is not patched in a future C# version, then I think what this PR does might be fine.

Regarding Unity, do they actually guarantee that the fields will be initialized with a non-null value, or do they need validity checks like this PR?
